================================================================================
                     CISCO CCNA - IOS COMMAND REFERENCE
                   SWITCHING | ROUTING | TROUBLESHOOTING
================================================================================
Format: Cisco IOS CLI / CMD-style quick reference
Applies to: Common Cisco IOS/IOS XE switches and routers. Availability and
interface names vary by platform, IOS version, and Packet Tracer device.

CLI MODES / PROMPTS
-------------------
Switch>                  User EXEC
Switch#                  Privileged EXEC
Switch(config)#          Global configuration
Switch(config-if)#       Interface configuration
Switch(config-vlan)#     VLAN configuration
Switch(config-line)#     Line configuration

NOTATION
--------
<value> = replace placeholder; do not type brackets.
!       = IOS configuration comment (in examples only).
#       = privileged EXEC prompt, not part of command.
Use 'do <show command>' to run EXEC commands from configuration mode.
Use '?' for contextual help, TAB for completion, Ctrl+Z to return to EXEC.

================================================================================
01. BASIC NAVIGATION AND SAVE
================================================================================
Switch> enable
Switch# configure terminal
Switch(config)# hostname SW1
SW1(config)# end
SW1# show running-config
SW1# show startup-config
SW1# show version
SW1# show inventory
SW1# show interfaces description
SW1# copy running-config startup-config
SW1# write memory                         ! Common alternative
SW1# show clock
SW1# terminal length 0                    ! Disable pagination for session
SW1# configure terminal
SW1(config)# no ip domain-lookup           ! Stop DNS lookup on mistyped commands
SW1(config)# end

NOTE: Save configuration from privileged EXEC (#), not interface config mode.
      Use 'end' or Ctrl+Z before 'copy running-config startup-config'.

================================================================================
02. MAC ADDRESS TABLE (LAYER 2)
================================================================================
SW1# show mac address-table
SW1# show mac address-table dynamic
SW1# show mac address-table aging-time
SW1# show mac address-table dynamic address 0077.88ee.99ff
SW1# show mac address-table dynamic interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
SW1# show mac address-table interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
SW1# show mac address-table vlan 100
SW1# clear mac address-table dynamic       ! Clears learned dynamic entries
SW1# show interfaces status

TIP: MAC aging time is often 300 seconds by default, but check the switch.
     MAC address format used here: xxxx.xxxx.xxxx (Cisco dotted format).

================================================================================
03. ARP AND IP NEIGHBORS (LAYER 3)
================================================================================
R1# show ip arp
R1# show arp
R1# show ip arp | include 192.168.1.10
R1# clear arp-cache                        ! Platform-dependent behavior
R1# show ipv6 neighbors

TIP: IOS ARP timeout on many Ethernet L3 interfaces defaults to 4 hours
     (14,400 seconds), but it is configurable and platform-dependent.

================================================================================
04. VLAN - CREATE, VERIFY, ASSIGN, REMOVE
================================================================================
SW1# show vlan brief
SW1# show vlan id 100
SW1# show interfaces gigabitEthernet 0/1 switchport

CREATE VLAN 100 (NAME: RH)
--------------------------
Switch> enable
Switch# configure terminal
Switch(config)# vlan 100
Switch(config-vlan)# name RH
Switch(config-vlan)# exit
Switch(config)# end
Switch# copy running-config startup-config

ASSIGN VLAN 100 TO TWO ACCESS PORTS
-----------------------------------
SW1# configure terminal
SW1(config)# interface range fastEthernet 0/1 - 2
SW1(config-if-range)# switchport mode access
SW1(config-if-range)# switchport access vlan 100
SW1(config-if-range)# spanning-tree portfast
SW1(config-if-range)# end
SW1# show vlan brief
SW1# show interfaces fastEthernet 0/1 switchport

REMOVE VLAN (USE THE NUMERIC VLAN ID)
------------------------------------
SW1# configure terminal
SW1(config)# no vlan 100
SW1(config)# end

IMPORTANT: 'no vlan <name>' is not the normal IOS syntax; use 'no vlan <id>'.
           Deleting a VLAN can disrupt ports assigned to it. Reassign first.
           PortFast is for end-device access ports, not switch-to-switch links.

================================================================================
05. TRUNK / NATIVE VLAN / DTP
================================================================================
SW1# show interfaces trunk
SW1# show interfaces gigabitEthernet 0/1 switchport
SW1# show dtp interface gigabitEthernet 0/1     ! If supported

CONFIGURE STATIC 802.1Q TRUNK
-----------------------------
SW1# configure terminal
SW1(config)# vlan 99
SW1(config-vlan)# name NATIVE
SW1(config-vlan)# exit
SW1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
SW1(config-if)# switchport mode trunk
SW1(config-if)# switchport trunk native vlan 99
SW1(config-if)# switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,99,100
SW1(config-if)# switchport nonegotiate
SW1(config-if)# end
SW1# show interfaces trunk

DTP (ON PLATFORMS THAT SUPPORT IT)
----------------------------------
SW1(config-if)# switchport mode dynamic desirable
SW1(config-if)# switchport mode dynamic auto

TRUNK EDITING
-------------
SW1(config-if)# switchport trunk allowed vlan add 200
SW1(config-if)# switchport trunk allowed vlan remove 20
SW1(config-if)# no switchport trunk native vlan

CAUTION: Match native VLAN and allowed VLAN configurations at BOTH ends.
         Dynamic desirable is a DTP negotiation mode, not a recommended
         default for security-sensitive deployments. Prefer static trunks.
         Some older switches require 'switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q'
         before enabling trunk mode; many newer ones do not support that command.

